  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "0jie1b6",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "DIRECTIVE PRINCIPLES OF STATE POLICY (DPSP)"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Directive Principles of State Policy (DPSP) are enumerated in Part IV of the Indian Constitution, spanning Articles 36 to 51.",
        "DPSPs are non-justiciable in nature, meaning they are not legally enforceable by courts for their violation.",
        "The Constitution does not formally classify DPSPs, but on ideological grounds, they are categorized into Socialistic, Gandhian, and Liberal-Intellectual principles.",
        "The primary objective of DPSP is to establish social and economic democracy and create a welfare state.",
        "Important Supreme Court judgments, including the Minerva Mills case (1980), established that the Indian Constitution is founded on the bedrock of balance between Fundamental Rights and Directive Principles."
      ],
      "id": "ml9ez23"
    },
